TROY TOMAN: So just to give you
some context, as I mentioned

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.150
before, our initial
mission in the world

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.150
was to try to capture
the Earth every day

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.940
and make that accessible
to as many people

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.130
as we possibly could.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.135
And we're now kind of
moving-- we've accomplished

00:03:27.135 --> 00:03:29.260
that first mission, we're
getting the daily imagery

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.072
of the planet.

00:03:30.072 --> 00:03:31.780
But now we're kind of
moving up the stack

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.580
and realizing that
what people want

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.650
is actually data and
information, not just pictures.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.040
And so they don't actually
want a picture of every spot

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:39.998
on the Earth every day.

00:03:39.998 --> 00:03:42.690
They want to know, where there
are buildings, where there

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.470
are roads, when
things are changing,

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.892
when disasters have struck.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.350
And so increasingly,
we're starting

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.010
to build an analytics layer
on top of our data engine,

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.120
and feeding that
into our customers.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.450
So our platform, or the stack--

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.906
when we talk about full
stack, it goes all the way

00:03:57.906 --> 00:03:59.530
from embedded computers
that are flying

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.590
500 kilometers above the earth.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.120
So we build and run our
own ground stations.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.860
We obviously have to
store, and manage,

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.310
and process tens of terabytes
of data every single day.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:12.940
And then that all feeds back
through our user system.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.910
We do that mostly on
the Google Cloud today.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.089
It starts out with about
150 operational satellites,

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:22.480
the largest operational
constellation in the world.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.200
We have, I think, over a
dozen, maybe close to 20 ground

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.960
stations around the world
that receive the data.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.270
We upload that into the cloud.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.690
We have tens of thousands of
VMs that sit in Google Cloud

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.430
that crunch on that data.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:37.990
And then an API,
and sort of series

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.480
of ways for people to
access that information.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.380
To kind of dive down
a little bit more,

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.390
from a software
perspective, this data

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:49.500
comes into our
processing engine.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.140
So this is all the stuff that
kind of happens now in GCP.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.681
But you'll hear me talk a little
bit about our data pipeline,

00:04:54.681 --> 00:04:56.420
which is where we started.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.257
And that really is about
getting our imagery

00:04:58.257 --> 00:05:00.340
and doing all the work
that has to be done to make

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.230
that usable by customers.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:04.960
For example, when you take
an image of the earth,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.800
you've got a 2D image of what's
fundamentally a 3D surface.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.980
And we have to take and
essentially map every pixel

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:15.070
that we take from space onto a
physical location on the earth,

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.840
because we want accuracy
within three meters.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.980
So we have to know first--
you can have a slightly off

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.467
telemetry in space
that can cause

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:24.550
you to be 50 to 100 miles
off of where you thought

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:25.630
you were of the earth.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.129
So there's a whole
set of processing

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:30.146
that has to go to make that
useful to our customers.

00:05:30.146 --> 00:05:32.770
And then it turns out everybody
wants it in a different format.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.050
People want web tiles, people
want analytic products,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:36.430
people want
beautiful images that

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:38.800
look good that are
maybe stitched together,

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.380
mosaics of lots of
different images together.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.240
And so all that sort
of number crunching

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.310
goes on in the background
of our platform.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:47.390
So we do this.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.000
You know, I think
we have somewhere

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.830
