So obviously we had
to do some things

00:11:16.261 --> 00:11:18.800
where we had to take
Ansible abstractions for AWS

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.860
and substitute them with
Ansible extractions for Google.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:25.230
We had to go do a few
even commits to upstream.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.370
You know, I think I
did a commit to Packer

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.930
to support some advanced
Google Cloud features,

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:32.780
because we are crazy people
that like to use stuff

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.630
in alpha and Google Cloud.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:38.720
I'll tell a story
about that later.

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:40.340
But those open source
tools were there

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:44.060
and we leveraged them heavily
to move forward on the platform

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:45.277
as we went.

00:11:45.277 --> 00:11:47.360
And it really served as a
pretty strong foundation

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.950
for making the migration
go relatively quickly.

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:53.430
So the phase one piece was the
storage and imagery platform.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:56.090
And so we moved storage over.

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:58.270
It consists largely
of all the imagery

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:00.740
stored in GCS, just as images.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.230
We don't do any special sort
of database for those images.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.954
We store the blobs in GCS.

00:12:05.954 --> 00:12:07.370
The sort of, secret
sauce I guess,

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:10.610
comes into play with how
we manage all the metadata.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:12.470
So we keep track
of which satellite

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:15.470
it came from, where we took
the picture, what rectification

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:17.180
coefficients we need,
what color curves

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.940
we've applied to the image,
all that type of data

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:22.770
over the history of
the lifetime we keep.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:25.850
And that used to be stored
in a Postgres database,

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.545
primarily because
of PostGIS, which

00:12:27.545 --> 00:12:31.790
is the geospatial information
extensions to Postgres.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.420
And so we moved a 20 terabyte--

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:35.150
I think a 10 or 20 terabyte--

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.772
Postgres database
over in the process.

00:12:37.772 --> 00:12:39.230
Since we've been
on Google, that we

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:41.690
then migrated a lot of that
metadata out of Postgres

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.360
into a Bigtable.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:45.820
But we did that as
a two step process

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.250
that worked very well for
us, and I'll talk about that.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.360
Our jobs platform, really, is
just a batch queuing platform

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:54.760
that manages a pool of VMs.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:57.340
And I'll give you an idea
of what that pool looks like

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.470
and how we scale that over time.

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:00.845
And then phase
two, which is what

00:13:00.845 --> 00:13:02.386
we're in the middle
of now, is really

00:13:02.386 --> 00:13:04.000
all of our container
based services.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.320
So we moved all of
these public facing APIs

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:08.480
into containers,
onto Kubernetes,

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.860
self-hosted Kubernetes on
AWS, and then what we've done

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.520
is built a similar
platform on Google,

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.560
and then allowed people
to basically repoint

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.080
their deployments from a
Kubernetes environmen in AWS

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.690
to a Kubernetes
environment in Google.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:27.460
We also make heavy use of
Cloud SQL, mostly Postgres.

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:30.520
And well, we have a lot
of Elasticsearch in here.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:32.020
But I don't want
to talk about that,

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:33.895
because anybody that
does operations probably

00:13:33.895 --> 00:13:35.790
doesn't like to talk
about Elasticsearch.

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:37.206
I'll answer questions
if you want.

00:13:37.206 --> 00:13:39.900
But we do have a couple of very
large Elasticsearch back ends

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.940
for all of this as well.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.300
So with that in mind, of the
background of what we did,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.910
I'm going to let Rajesh
talk a bit about some

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:49.517
of the monitoring
ideas that I think

00:13:49.517 --> 00:13:51.600
we both have a philosophy
for, but he has way more

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:52.310
experience with.

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:53.560
RAJESH RAMAN: Thank you, Troy.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.160
So I'd like to talk a
little bit about how

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.860
you go about monitoring
something as complicated

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:02.940
as migrating from one
environment to another.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:04.482
But the first thing
I want to raise

00:14:04.482 --> 00:14:06.690
is, that when you're
considering such a large change,

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:09.450
you should really think
strategically about monitoring.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.820
So obviously making a
change in cloud environments

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:12.507
is a big change.

00:14:12.507 --> 00:14:14.340
But if you think about
it over the lifecycle

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:17.880
of your entire service or
your entire product, you know,

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.160
that's only one of the
changes that your system

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:21.900
has to be able to handle.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:23.340
You'll obviously
be making changes

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:24.990
to various kinds
of implementations,

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:27.420
various kinds of
architectures, new builds, even

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:29.166
things like
configuration changes.

00:14:29.166 --> 00:14:30.540
And these are all
things that you

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:33.330
will have to be
able to monitor over

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:35.850
the life cycle of your service.

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:38.250
Monitoring is not only about
observing system behavior,

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:39.669
but also understanding it.

00:14:39.669 --> 00:14:41.460
You really want to have
insight into what's

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.377
happening inside your
service, not just seeing

00:14:43.377 --> 00:14:45.835
that it's, OK, it's processing
some number of transactions,

00:14:45.835 --> 00:14:47.400
but what's it doing
under the covers?

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:49.390
So that you know if
something goes wrong,

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.960
what's actually
happening under the hood.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.090
And so when you're doing
something as complicated

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:56.370
as migrating from one
environment to another,

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:59.300
you want to ensure that you have
the right monitoring strategy

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:01.735
way before you actually
start doing the migration.

00:15:01.735 --> 00:15:03.360
So you saw that Planet
actually started

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:07.890
using SignalFX a couple of
years before they actually

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:09.804
finished their migration.

00:15:09.804 --> 00:15:11.220
And the reason you
want to do this

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:12.990
is, you want to
move as few things,

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:15.480
you want to change as
few things as possible

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:16.980
when you actually
do this migration.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.630
You want to establish
all the baselines

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:21.480
and have all the observability
that you need way before you

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:23.280
actually execute the migration.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.910
And it's a good opportunity to
use your upcoming GCP migration

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.210
as a forcing function to
ensure that you're putting all

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:33.360
the right monitoring best
practices in your organization,

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:35.100
the right philosophy
or observability,

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:38.150
and having the right tooling
and the right products in place

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:39.900
for you to ensure that
you understand what

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.490
your system is actually doing.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:44.352
So the first class,
observability,

00:15:44.352 --> 00:15:45.810
once you have it,
is not only going

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.550
to help you manage
this migration,

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.010
but it'll help you operate
your service after you

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.570
complete the migration.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:57.810
So a few lessons to share
about monitoring in general.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:15:59.550
So there's much
more to monitoring

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:02.190
than just infrastructure,
especially when you're

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:05.050
moving from one cloud
system to another.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:09.340
So for one thing, like system
metrics are not very useful.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.602
They are often not
even comparable across

00:16:11.602 --> 00:16:12.810
different cloud environments.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.330
Because for example, what's
an instance type in AWS

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.810
might not map exactly
to what you have in GCP.

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:20.490
And so it doesn't
really make sense

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:22.550
to compare things that
aren't really comparable.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:24.180
It's like apples and oranges.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.520
But there are some more
systematic problems

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:28.320
with using just system metrics.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.780
So often times,
system metrics do not

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.820
indicate application
performance issues.

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:34.844
You know, your load
averages might look fine.

00:16:34.844 --> 00:16:36.510
Your memory utilization
might look fine.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:37.968
But your application
might actually

00:16:37.968 --> 00:16:40.840
be struggling for
various other reasons.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:43.680
And even when system metrics
do indicate system performance

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.412
issues, they often are not
very actionable in the sense,

00:16:46.412 --> 00:16:48.870
they don't tell you what the
problem is, they just tell you

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:51.090
that there is a problem.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:54.270
So you really need to go
beyond system metrics.

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:57.210
You want to take-- what we
call-- a data driven approach

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:58.900
to development and operations.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:01.680
The key insight here is
that you cannot understand,

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.339
you cannot improve, you cannot
manage what you don't measure.

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:08.250
So you really need to think
about measuring all the things

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.619
that matter to you
as a person who's

00:17:10.619 --> 00:17:11.940
developing an application.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.849
What do you characterize
as the proper behavior

00:17:14.849 --> 00:17:16.471
of that application?

00:17:16.471 --> 00:17:18.720
Even if you have a really
simple service, for example,

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.910
it might be some kind
of a look-up service,

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.535
you get a request to
look up something,

00:17:22.535 --> 00:17:24.826
you look it up in the cache,
if it's there in the cache

00:17:24.826 --> 00:17:25.599
you can return it.

00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:27.599
If it's not in the cache,
you put it into a queue,

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:30.182
and maybe there's a third pool
that pulls stuff off the queue,

00:17:30.182 --> 00:17:32.229
looks it up in the database,
and then returns it.

00:17:32.229 --> 00:17:34.770
Well, you should be measuring,
not only the number of request

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:35.730
that you're getting,
but you should

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:38.146
be measuring the number of
cache hits, the number of cache

00:17:38.146 --> 00:17:40.201
misses, what's the size
of your queue, what's

00:17:40.201 --> 00:17:41.700
the size of your
thread pool, what's

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:43.440
the utilization on
this thread pool?

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.330
Each of these
instrumentation points

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.040
is hardly like one
or two lines of code.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:48.960
But believe me, it'll
pay off in spades

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:52.290
when something falls in the pot.

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:56.040
So if you're in
doubt, instrument, OK?

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:58.830
So the wrong time to wish
you had instrumentation

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.580
is when you're in the
middle of an outage,

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:02.079
it's 3:00 AM in the
morning, and you

00:18:02.079 --> 00:18:04.650
wish that you knew what was
actually happening over there.

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:05.760
You see that things
are running slow,

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:08.343
but you don't know if it's the
database, or if it's the cache,

00:18:08.343 --> 00:18:10.590
or what is the
problem right there.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:14.730
So a fairly moderately
instrumented application,

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:16.150
you know, of
moderate complexity,

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:17.880
it's not unusual for
them to even send

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.960
thousands of metrics per host.

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:23.524
It's not unusual.

00:18:23.524 --> 00:18:24.940
But once you do
that, if you have,

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:26.716
let's say, 1,000
metrics coming per host

00:18:26.716 --> 00:18:29.090
and you have thousands or tens
of thousands of instances,

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:31.810
you are now looking at
millions of time series.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:34.910
And you can't really absorb
that kind of information.

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:37.780
So you really need to use,
then, analytics to boil

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:41.530
all this information down
into a small number of KPIs

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:43.000
that you can actually absorb.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.130
So obviously, you
need to be measuring

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:49.180
the KPIs, which are what
you promise as a service

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:49.950
to your clients.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:54.160
So these are the SLAs, the
SLOs, the SLIs that clients

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:55.764
of your service care about.

00:18:55.764 --> 00:18:57.430
You obviously need
to be measuring that,

00:18:57.430 --> 00:18:59.290
because otherwise you cannot
guarantee that you're actually

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:01.180
meeting any of those objectives.

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.820
But equally, you should
also be measuring the things

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:07.650
that you, as an application
writer, care about, right?

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:10.150
you should be-- for example,
if it was that look-up service,

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:11.920
I would want to
know what the hit

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:14.660
ratio, what's the aggregate
hit ratio across the entirety?

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:18.160
Or what's the 99th
percentile of the queue sizes

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:19.690
across my entire tier?

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:23.052
What's the thread utilization
on my thread pools?

00:19:23.052 --> 00:19:24.760
So there are some
small number of metrics

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:28.370
that will capture what's
happening in your system.

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:31.129
If you think about a pilot who's
flying a complicated airplane,

00:19:31.129 --> 00:19:32.920
there's obviously a
lot of metrics that are

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:34.330
available to that pilot, right?

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:36.787
Everything from like various
hydraulic pressures, and oil

00:19:36.787 --> 00:19:39.370
pressures, and oil temperatures,
and exhaust gas temperatures,

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:40.161
and all that stuff.

00:19:40.161 --> 00:19:42.436
But that's not what's
on the pilot's HUD.

00:19:42.436 --> 00:19:43.810
The pilot is
operating the system

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:45.700
based on a small number of
metrics that are projected

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:47.020
onto the head up display.

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:48.520
And yes, if something
happens, then

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:49.811
yes, they are available to you.

00:19:49.811 --> 00:19:52.880
And those other metrics are
available to debug and fix

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:53.380
the problem.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:55.990
And you need to take that
same sort of mentality

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:59.430
when you're operating
your own applications.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:02.840
So use analytics to take all
this complicated information

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.300
and boil them down to a
small number of metrics

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.090
that you're actually monitoring.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:10.010
Now second, use your
monitoring tools

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:12.000
to establish a baseline
for system behavior.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.070
So this is one of
the reasons why

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:15.236
you want to set up your
monitoring and observability

00:20:15.236 --> 00:20:17.870
tool-chain way before you
actually do the migration.

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:20.040
What is it normal
for your system?

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:24.320
It's not always a very
simple question to answer.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:28.269
So here's a chart of activity in
one of SignalFx's own systems.

00:20:28.269 --> 00:20:30.310
And you can see that it's
very strongly periodic.

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:31.712
This is activity over a week.

00:20:31.712 --> 00:20:33.170
So you can see that
in the weekdays

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:34.836
there's a lot of
activity on the system.

00:20:34.836 --> 00:20:36.950
During the weekends,
it's not so active.

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:38.940
And so, if somebody
came up to me and asked,

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:41.960
hey, Rajesh, is 30,000 normal?

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.230
Well, 30,000 is not normal
if it's Sunday afternoon.

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:47.360
It's also not normal
if it's Monday, noon.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:50.480
But it is about normal if
it's Monday at 9:00 AM.

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:52.850
So what is normal would
depend a lot on your system.

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:54.933
So like, this system is,
of course, very dependent

00:20:54.933 --> 00:20:56.660
on weekly behavior.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:58.880
But you can imagine other
systems that we have--

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.920
for example, there
might be a warm up phase

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:03.602
when you push a service that
your caches all get cold,

00:21:03.602 --> 00:21:05.810
and so it takes some time
for your caches to warm up.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:07.952
And that might be
normal for your system.

00:21:07.952 --> 00:21:10.160
There might be other things
where it's very periodic,

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:12.240
but on a monthly basis,
or on a quarterly basis,

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:14.240
or based on other
[? reasons of ?] activity that

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:15.680
might happen in your system.

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:17.180
So you want to make
sure that you're

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:18.470
capturing all these
metrics and you

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:20.210
have enough baseline
in them that when

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:23.300
you do your migration, you
have something to compare with.

00:21:23.300 --> 00:21:24.292
If you don't have--

00:21:24.292 --> 00:21:26.000
if you're not giving
yourself enough time

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:27.536
to establish that
baseline, you're

00:21:27.536 --> 00:21:29.160
not going to be able
to, again, compare

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:31.243
and know that if your
[? migrated ?] instances are

00:21:31.243 --> 00:21:33.560
kind of doing something
that is normal.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:35.570
And of course, comparing
it to past behavior

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:37.677
is one notion of what's normal.

00:21:37.677 --> 00:21:40.260
You might also want to compare
it to your other control group.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:44.039
So if you're migrating
from let's say, AWS to GCP,

00:21:44.039 --> 00:21:45.830
and you have some
instances running in GCP,

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:48.870
you want to measure apples
to apples comparison of what

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:51.065
is happening on GCP versus
what's happening on AWS.

00:21:51.065 --> 00:21:53.690
And again, here, you don't want
to be doing the system metrics,

00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:56.420
you want to be doing the KPIs
and the other metrics that

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:59.030
really give you a proper
comparison if your system is

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:00.050
working well.

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:02.360
Now this whole a
versus b is not just

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:04.130
like GCP versus AWS, right?

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:06.810
It can be, oh, here
it is my canary build,

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:09.230
or here's an alternate
implementation of an algorithm

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:11.060
which I think is going
to be much better.

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:11.930
But is it better?

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.055
You should be measuring it.

00:22:13.055 --> 00:22:15.388
That's the only way you're
going to know if you actually

00:22:15.388 --> 00:22:16.880
made an improvement or not.

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:19.190
And not just measuring it
in a synthetic benchmark.

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:21.290
You want to be testing
it in production

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:23.804
to make sure that it's working
as you intend it to work.

00:22:23.804 --> 00:22:25.970
In this case, we actually
pushed out a canary build.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:27.511
And you can see that
the canary build

00:22:27.511 --> 00:22:31.220
is working very, very different
than the rest of the population

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:32.420
of the servers.

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.580
In this case, it was
actually an optimization,

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:36.810
so doing as it was intended.

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:38.990
If it was not, we
would have caught it

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:41.510
within a few seconds or minutes
and reverted the canary.

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:48.430
So the third thing is
that timeliness of metrics

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:53.200
and analytics is critical, OK?

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:56.200
So Micrososervices,
data sampling frequency,

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:58.480
there's a lot that can
happen within the span

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:00.016
of a few minutes.

00:23:00.016 --> 00:23:01.390
Most of you are
familiar with all

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:03.380
these microservice architecture.

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:05.556
These are very large,
complicated dynamic systems.

00:23:05.556 --> 00:23:07.930
They're growing and shrinking,
they're auto-provisioning,

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:10.450
they're auto-scaling, they
are doing a lot of stuff.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.890
And a lot can happen within
a span of a few minutes.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:16.000
So you may fail to identify
patterns and activity

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:19.630
by sampling, kind of like
coarse grained metrics,

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:21.939
or things that very,
very high latency.

00:23:21.939 --> 00:23:23.980
Just imagine the [INAUDIBLE]
to continue, kind of

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:25.540
like the aviation metaphor.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:27.760
Imagine a pilot trying
to fly an airplane where

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:30.280
all the instruments updated
once a minute instead

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:32.560
of every time they made
a change to the controls.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:34.860
But that's what most people
are trying to do nowadays.

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:36.651
They are trying to
operate these very, very

00:23:36.651 --> 00:23:39.070
large complicated systems
by looking at metrics that

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:40.880
operate like once a minute.

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:43.390
So low latency real time
monitoring really, really

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:45.407
lets you understand
how the system behaves

00:23:45.407 --> 00:23:47.740
when you make changes to them
and you push a canary when

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:49.240
you update a configuration.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:50.807
How does your
system really behave

00:23:50.807 --> 00:23:52.765
to be able to identify
the patterns that happen

00:23:52.765 --> 00:23:55.030
and to be able to react
very quickly when something

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:56.524
is happening that
you don't expect?

00:23:56.524 --> 00:23:58.690
Or something that you don't
understand, or something

00:23:58.690 --> 00:24:00.310
that should not be
happening, to be

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:02.600
able to identify those
behaviors and react to them,

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:06.670
remedy the problem, and verify
that your remediations are

00:24:06.670 --> 00:24:08.150
actually working.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.390
So, you know, the way I
like to think about it

00:24:10.390 --> 00:24:14.740
is that your system is telling
a very complicated story.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:17.590
And you should be watching the
movie and not a slide show.

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:20.380
So this is the value of real
time streaming analytics

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:24.970
in terms of having ingesting
higher resolution, low latency

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:30.150
data, and giving you analytic
insight instantaneously.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:34.010
So to summarize, make sure that
you go beyond infrastructure

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:34.880
level metrics.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:37.220
Measure all the things
that matter to your system.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:38.960
And if you're in doubt, measure.

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:42.420
It's always easy to throw
data out if you don't need it.

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:43.796
But it's impossible to--

00:24:43.796 --> 00:24:45.170
you can wish as
much as you want,

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:47.295
you're not going to get
the instrumentation in when

00:24:47.295 --> 00:24:50.120
you need it when things
are falling apart.

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:51.720
Use analytics,
because at this point,

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:52.820
you're going to have
a whole lot of data.

00:24:52.820 --> 00:24:54.260
You really need
analytics to help

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:57.150
you identify aggregate
system behavior,

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:01.460
identify outliers, deviations
from normal behavior,

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:04.340
and establish baselines
for system behavior,

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:06.990
and measure in real time.

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.990
Ingest at the highest
possible resolution

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:11.150
with the lowest
possible latency for you

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:13.490
to kind of operate your
system successfully.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:16.310
So Troy is now going to talk
us through how Planet Labs did

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:19.132
their migration.

00:25:19.132 --> 00:25:21.590
TROY TOMAN: So just to kind of
touch on some of the metrics

00:25:21.590 --> 00:25:24.290
that we've used, first
of all, I apologize,

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:27.290
a lot of the migration we did
predated the year [? back. ?]

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:28.940
I can look in the
graphs that we have.

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:31.220
So most of the graphs are
not right during the middle

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:31.928
of the migration.

00:25:31.928 --> 00:25:33.680
But I found some
that I think show

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:35.180
a lot of the kinds
of things that we

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:36.360
found during the migration.

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:37.930
And sort of how
we think about it.

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:40.440
So I'll dive into a few
of these more directly.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:43.130
So this is kind of just to give
you an idea of the environment

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:45.296
