00:27:20.870 --> 00:27:22.953
and then were able to
correct and sort of monitor.

00:27:22.953 --> 00:27:25.700
So just another example
of one of the small things

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:28.869
that we watch on our ops teams.

00:27:28.869 --> 00:27:30.410
This one, I think,
is just a good way

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:32.630
of how you can watch
changes through the system.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:35.120
And I do wish we could
sort of stretch this back

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:36.450
to the migration time frame.

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:39.020
But this is looking at some
metrics of our back end

00:27:39.020 --> 00:27:40.880
database server for
that storage environment

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:42.322
that I talked about.

00:27:42.322 --> 00:27:44.030
So this is an environment
that's tracking

00:27:44.030 --> 00:27:46.310
over two billion objects
and all the metadata that's

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:48.270
associated with that.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:51.860
So we moved it to Google and
learned some good lessons

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:54.690
about moving big databases
that I'll get to later.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.630
But one of the big things
that we wanted to do

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:59.264
was actually leverage
Bigtable to manage

00:27:59.264 --> 00:28:00.180
a lot of our metadata.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.210
So we had all the stuff
stored in Postgres

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:05.090
in a horribly inefficient
manner, like just big JSON

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:07.454
blobs instead of
a Postgres column.

00:28:07.454 --> 00:28:08.870
If you want to
have fun some time,

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:12.630
have 100K JSON blobs in Postgres
columns and try to manage that.

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:15.749
So we pulled all that data
out and put it into a Bigtable

00:28:15.749 --> 00:28:17.790
and flipped the switch on
that in an environment.

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.940
And again, to track
sort of what we wanted,

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:21.650
our network
utilization the day we

00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:23.960
flipped the Bigtable
switch just dropped.

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:26.210
Because all that data stopped
coming out of our system

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:27.960
and started coming out
a Bigtable instead.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:29.840
So this was just like,
one of those metrics

00:28:29.840 --> 00:28:32.900
that we could see what was
happening during that time

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:33.507
frame.

00:28:33.507 --> 00:28:35.090
Another thing that
we did is we went--

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:40.400
we used to have an on disk based
backup where we would run PD

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:42.230
dump to get our backups--

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:45.110
PD based backup
to do our pieces.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:49.520
But we switched over to actually
mounting an extra Persistent

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:51.770
Disk and snapshotting the
database to Persistent Disk

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:53.630
and then moving that
Persistent Disk.

00:28:53.630 --> 00:28:57.440
And you'll see that new backup
popped in in our disk activity.

00:28:57.440 --> 00:29:00.407
So this is actually the instance
where we do all of our backups.

00:29:00.407 --> 00:29:02.240
And so we were getting
lots of disk activity

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.905
until we did the snapshot stuff,
and that sort of dropped off.

00:29:04.905 --> 00:29:07.280
And then I guess, the last
thing that sort of shows here,

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:09.002
is we actually--

00:29:09.002 --> 00:29:10.460
one of the things
about Postgres is

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:13.280
if you drop a
Bigtable or a column

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:16.520
in our case that was all this
JSON data, the storage for that

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:18.429
doesn't go away immediately.

00:29:18.429 --> 00:29:19.970
You actually have
to dump and restore

00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:21.500
the database to get it away.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:23.990
So the performance came down
in the network bandwidth.

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:26.240
But that was all-- the
disk utilization was still

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:27.720
there in the system.

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:29.720
So we decided we would
build a brand new--

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:32.360
go from a [? 9.3 ?] server
to a [? 9.5 ?] server,

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:34.040
do a dump and import
of the database.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:38.540
And as things sort of happened,
this was the first try.

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:41.470
It didn't work, which is kind
of why we see some spikes.

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:43.467
And then everything
goes back to normal.

00:29:43.467 --> 00:29:45.050
But we figured out
why it didn't work.

00:29:45.050 --> 00:29:47.091
And a few months later,
pulled the trigger again,

00:29:47.091 --> 00:29:48.440
and sort of got to watch.

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:50.300
We'll see a new
database server, but we

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:51.930
were able to do a before
and after comparison

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:53.120
of the old to the
new to make sure

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:55.362
that the statistics lined
up and certainly didn't get

00:29:55.362 --> 00:29:56.570
any worse than it did before.

00:29:56.570 --> 00:29:58.460
So I think it just
sort of-- you know,

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:00.590
this is obviously over a
very long period of time.

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:02.173
So it's not what we
use operationally,

00:30:02.173 --> 00:30:03.562
but it's great to
tell the story.

00:30:03.562 --> 00:30:05.270
Or maybe this is our
version of the movie

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:08.270
of what happens to big
Postgres databases at Planet.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:10.184
It is very useful for us.

00:30:10.184 --> 00:30:12.350
Another big thing that we
get out of our environment

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:16.500
that we love is
the fact that we--

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:19.610
our AWS platform, our
monitoring and visibility

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:21.917
looks exactly like our GCP.

00:30:21.917 --> 00:30:24.500
So I mentioned that we're moving
these Kubernetes environments

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:25.260
back and forth.

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:28.670
So we use an internal
tooling system called K2

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:30.910
to deploy Kubernetes into AWS.

00:30:30.910 --> 00:30:33.131
And that K2 platform,
we name all of-- again,

00:30:33.131 --> 00:30:35.630
we're really good at naming--
we name all of our clusters KK

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:37.220
something.

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:40.250
In Google, we move
to using Terraform

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:41.490
to deploy all of our stuff.

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:42.865
We actually got
kind of creative,

00:30:42.865 --> 00:30:45.110
we call that tool Terraflop,
which is a little more

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:46.235
interesting than Terraform.

00:30:46.235 --> 00:30:49.340
But it's all TFK.

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:52.802
So you'll see up here, we
get a common view of both--

00:30:52.802 --> 00:30:54.260
and you know, our
Google cluster is

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:56.060
right next to our
AWS clusters as we're

00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:57.800
sort of walking through
this environment.

00:30:57.800 --> 00:30:58.980
And it goes even deeper.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:01.340
This is an example of
our dashboard for one

00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:02.030
of our clusters.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:03.740
So this is a Google cluster.

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:06.680
These are the variety of metrics
that are our standard ops guys

00:31:06.680 --> 00:31:07.580
use.

00:31:07.580 --> 00:31:10.632
But this looks exactly the
same for our AWS clusters

00:31:10.632 --> 00:31:12.090
as it does for our
Google clusters.

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:14.540
So if we get a page in the
middle of the night and-- we

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:17.049
don't get those, but
I hear people do--

00:31:17.049 --> 00:31:18.590
when they wake up
at 3:00 AM, they're

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:21.290
not having to go like, oh,
which environment I am,

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:22.890
and what signals
am I looking for?

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:25.040
They see a lot of consistency.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:27.890
I will say the one difference--
if I showed you the snapshot--

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:31.370
is, those two top lines
would be blank in AWS

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:34.950
because we've moved to a service
mesh [? linker ?] [? e-based ?]

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:38.259
ingress and we don't
have that in AWS.

00:31:38.259 --> 00:31:39.800
But we've left the
format the same so

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:43.640
that the visibility to the
ops guys is also consistent.

00:31:46.079 --> 00:31:47.370
So that is kind of an overview.

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:48.960
I thought what maybe
the most valuable thing,

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:50.543
rather than get into
the nitty gritty,

00:31:50.543 --> 00:31:53.355
or at least maybe provide some
context for the nitty gritty,

00:31:53.355 --> 00:31:55.230
is to share with you
some things that I think

00:31:55.230 --> 00:31:58.280
we learned at Planet
about doing a migration

00:31:58.280 --> 00:31:59.210
of this kind of scale.

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:01.470
Both about how to do them,
and maybe some things

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:04.110
about Google Cloud
in the process.

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.630
So my number one
recommendation to anybody

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:09.390
doing a migration like this
is, don't change anything

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:12.210
you don't have to change.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:14.779
You know, well, so I'll
come back to that second.

00:32:14.779 --> 00:32:16.320
So I guess, the
first thing actually,

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:19.075
before that, I sometimes
take this for granted

00:32:19.075 --> 00:32:21.680
and I almost forgot we
put this in the slide.

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:24.510
But the planning part
is actually essential.

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:26.002
We were in a hurry.

00:32:26.002 --> 00:32:27.960
But we took a lot of time
to evaluate the move,

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:29.790
make sure it was the right move.

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:32.490
Maybe more importantly,
we locked ourselves

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:34.680
into a conference
room in San Antonio

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:37.590
for like a week to think of all
the things that could go wrong,

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:40.140
and how we wanted to do this,
and have a lot of debates.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.720
That time up front, even though
we felt a lot of pressure

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:45.345
to move fast was
invaluable in the process.

00:32:45.345 --> 00:32:48.060
So don't shortchange yourself on
thinking about this stuff ahead

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:50.880
of time, and asking all
the crazy questions, which

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:53.100
will never be all the
questions you will find.

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:56.234
But you'll find that some of
those were really helpful.

00:32:56.234 --> 00:32:57.650
The second thing,
and the one that

00:32:57.650 --> 00:32:59.025
probably rings
the truest for me,

00:32:59.025 --> 00:33:00.630
because we didn't
do this everywhere,

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:03.870
is change as few
things as possible.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.960
It's super tempting-- and we
fell into this in a couple

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:07.782
of places--

00:33:07.782 --> 00:33:09.240
but to want to fix
all the problems

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:11.545
of your service and your
app while you're migrating.

00:33:11.545 --> 00:33:13.920
You know, I don't-- oh, I'm
in an old version of Ansible,

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:14.700
I don't get to use that anymore.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:16.950
I want to get off a Postgres,
let's move to a Bigtable

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:18.661
while we move to Google.

00:33:18.661 --> 00:33:20.160
Let's containerize
everything, we'll

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:24.270
go from Ansible base VMs and AWS
to containerized new solution

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:25.920
and GCP.

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:28.290
Resist that temptation.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:31.170
It makes the migration
way more complicated.

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:33.480
And I guarantee you it will
take you three times longer

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.700
to move if you do
that than if you move

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.047
and then make the change.

00:33:38.047 --> 00:33:39.630
So my recommendation
is to avoid that.

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:42.480
So we moved the biggest part
of our infrastructure, probably

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:45.910
75% of our servers and
applications in 90 days.

00:33:45.910 --> 00:33:48.810
And the last 20% that
we're moving over

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:50.570
has taken us 18 months.

00:33:50.570 --> 00:33:52.320
Because it's gotten
much more predictable,

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:53.790
people are changing
lots of things.

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:56.248
The refactoring that they want
to do before they move never

00:33:56.248 --> 00:33:57.699
quite finishes.

00:33:57.699 --> 00:33:59.490
And it just sort of
drags out, because it's

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:03.600
much harder to bracket, I
think, things around that.

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:05.130
And that worked for us.

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:07.170
Like, I think in
particular, had we

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:09.780
tried to take our 20
terabyte Postgres database

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:13.230
and migrate to Bigtable
while we're going to GCP,

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.659
the problem is more
things are going to break

00:34:15.659 --> 00:34:17.639
and it's more
confusing what broke.

00:34:17.639 --> 00:34:20.250
Did it break because Bigtable
didn't work for application?

00:34:20.250 --> 00:34:22.374
Did it break because the
Google Cloud is different?

00:34:22.374 --> 00:34:24.174
Did it break because
our VPNs didn't work?

00:34:24.174 --> 00:34:26.340
Like, all of those things
just get more complicated.

00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:28.110
So I think in some
ways, it's kind

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:30.179
of the antithesis
of a micro services,

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:32.979
or maybe a CI sort of
incremental change approach.

00:34:32.979 --> 00:34:35.520
The more you [? bake ?] into
the change, the more complicated

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:37.270
it's going to be, the more
likely you're going to fail.

00:34:37.270 --> 00:34:39.186
And it's actually going
to take you more time.

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:45.326
Another big thing, you heard
me sort of talk about this,

00:34:45.326 --> 00:34:46.409
and I'll get some details.

00:34:46.409 --> 00:34:48.810
But data gravity is real.

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:51.000
It seems-- like if you're
looking at migrating,

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:53.790
particularly from
something like AWS to GCP--

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:56.100
it seems like the two most
similar things on the planet

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:57.830
are S3 and GCS.

00:34:57.830 --> 00:34:59.580
So you want-- it's
easy to just kind of go

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:01.163
like, oh, l got a
bunch of data in S3,

00:35:01.163 --> 00:35:04.560
I'm just going to
copy that data to GCS.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:07.329
The problem is, when
you start doing things--

00:35:07.329 --> 00:35:09.120
and not everybody does
this on this scale--

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:12.540
but when you start talking about
moving seven petabytes of data,

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:14.400
it turns out there's
not an O'Reilly

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:16.500
book that is very
good about how to move

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:18.180
seven petabytes of data.

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:20.250
So we started by
doing some tests.

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:22.620
I took a database
that was on AWS

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:26.130
and I did a base backup to GCP.

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:28.762
And I was totally blown away
by how fast the backup was

00:35:28.762 --> 00:35:30.720
and thought, wow, this
whole data copying thing

00:35:30.720 --> 00:35:32.880
is going to be easy.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:35.580
The problem is, one database
backup doesn't simulate

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:40.290
moving two billion objects from
GCS that was on in one bucket,

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:40.830
I would say.

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:42.496
And I can talk to you
about why that is.

00:35:42.496 --> 00:35:45.120
But we have bucket with
multi-billions of objects

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:47.340
that we were trying
to move over.

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:49.110
And so we did this testing.

00:35:49.110 --> 00:35:51.600
We thought the pipes are
big, because it turns out,

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:53.249
you know, the
advertisers and the ads

00:35:53.249 --> 00:35:55.290
are all sort of go in
between those two networks.

00:35:55.290 --> 00:35:57.690
Both Amazon and Google
are set up to do that.

00:35:57.690 --> 00:36:00.540
But, you know, it
turns out we used the--

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:02.760
there's a cloud migration
tool that Google has,

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:04.179
that's actually quite good.

00:36:04.179 --> 00:36:06.720
So we just like, we flipped that
on, we put it at the bucket,

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:08.803
we started running our
tests, and we're like, wow,

00:36:08.803 --> 00:36:11.670
we're going to be here for
six months if this happens.

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:13.830
And so we sat down
with the Google team,

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:14.872
and we figured out that--

00:36:14.872 --> 00:36:16.871
you know, part of it is
they're sort of serially

00:36:16.871 --> 00:36:18.990
going through our
keyspace object by object,

00:36:18.990 --> 00:36:21.590
doing these look-ups,
moving this data over.

00:36:21.590 --> 00:36:23.760
And so what we ended
up doing to make

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:25.980
a long story a
little bit shorter,

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:28.710
is that we worked with Google.

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:31.650
And how our thing was
set up and we ended up

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:36.870
running 32 separate simultaneous
migration jobs by dividing up

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:37.470
the keyspace.

00:36:37.470 --> 00:36:39.210
We had a pretty
distributed keyspace.

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:41.610
We use MD5 sums of
all of our images

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:43.110
to be the main storing place.

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:44.100
So it's wildly spread.

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:47.280
And we literally just took
that keyspace set, divided it

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:50.449
into 32 groups, and then
put those key spaces

00:36:50.449 --> 00:36:52.740
into 32 separate migration
jobs and flipped the switch.

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:56.070
And 21 days later, 7 and 1/2
petabytes of data was moved.

00:36:56.070 --> 00:36:57.920
So you can do it.

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:00.960
But you have to know
that it's probably--

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:03.240
like what we did doesn't
work for everybody else.

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:05.280
There's different
ways to approach it.

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:07.530
I would also say, probably
the other interesting story

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:12.300
that we had to move over
was the post-crash database.

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:13.710
So we have a self--

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:16.030
we have a series
of RDS databases,

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:18.180
but we also had these
larger databases

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:21.110
that were self-manage
Postgres instances.

00:37:21.110 --> 00:37:23.820
And we thought the
simple thing to de--

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:26.160
simple thing to
do-- would just be

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:28.290
able to create a read-only
replica of our Postgres

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:30.730
database in Google.

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:32.110
So we tried to do that.

00:37:32.110 --> 00:37:34.410
And one of the problems
that we ran into

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:36.640
was that we did
everything inside a plan

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:38.220
on private networks.

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:40.260
We have regulatory
requirements and other sort

00:37:40.260 --> 00:37:43.180
of security things that keeps
everything without a public IP.

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:46.230
So our databases in
AWS had no public IP.

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:48.210
So it was basically
a VPN connected.

00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:51.510
And we just couldn't get
latency throughput to keep up

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:55.440
with our database performance to
create a replica in GCP running

00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:57.480
across VPNs.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:38:00.180
What we ended up doing that
actually worked quite well,

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:04.380
was we took an Elastic
IP in AWS, assigned it

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:07.200
to the database that we
had, and ran a replication

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:10.560
across the public network
for the two or three weeks

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:12.492
where we were
replicating into GCP.

00:38:12.492 --> 00:38:13.950
And it turns out
the public network

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:16.710
latencies were as
constrained as VPNs.

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:18.960
We actually got that to work.

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.110
And so we were running
for a long time

00:38:21.110 --> 00:38:26.340
with a replica in GCP off of
our primary database in AWS.

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:29.130
The thing that we missed was,
we got so comfortable with that.

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:31.370
And I was going to
embarrass the guy

00:38:31.370 --> 00:38:32.880
on the team who was
here, because he

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:33.755
was going to show up.

00:38:33.755 --> 00:38:36.210
But he must have known that I
was going to talk about him.

00:38:36.210 --> 00:38:39.580
Anyway, he got sick,
our main DBA got sick.

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:41.820
We all took our eyes-- we
actually had a graph that

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:43.070
showed our replication stats--

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:44.850
we took our eye off the ball.

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:47.160
We basically got
everything moved over

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:49.500
and ready to flip the
switch from that being the--

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:51.150
[? replicator ?]
being the primary.

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:53.340
And we had had some network
latency over the weekend

00:38:53.340 --> 00:38:57.020
before we did it that caused our
other one to get out of sync.

00:38:57.020 --> 00:38:59.004
And we ended up having
to wait two extra days

00:38:59.004 --> 00:39:00.420
while the database
caught back up,

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:02.669
and we sort of got that
solved and flipped the switch.

00:39:02.669 --> 00:39:06.312
So we kind of did part of
the migration on a Friday,

00:39:06.312 --> 00:39:08.520
figured out it was going to
work, came back in Monday

00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:09.978
morning, the replica
had caught up,

00:39:09.978 --> 00:39:12.090
we flipped it, and went
the rest of the way.

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:14.670
Which was good, because Will
was on stage at Next the Tuesday

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:15.989
after that.

00:39:15.989 --> 00:39:17.280
But we got it all done on time.

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:19.740
So I just think the whole
data thing actually turned out

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:20.982
to be quite hard.

00:39:20.982 --> 00:39:22.440
We think about
things like Bigtable

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:24.450
and these systems that
are very different.

00:39:24.450 --> 00:39:25.530
But actually it's
the things that

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:27.405
look the same that aren't
quite the same that

00:39:27.405 --> 00:39:29.700
are the most complicated
parts about a move like this.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.184
So for us, I think if
you're coming from an AWS

00:39:35.184 --> 00:39:37.350
to Google environment, the
biggest challenges for us

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:39.582
were to understand networking.

00:39:39.582 --> 00:39:41.790
You know, you kind of think
networking is networking,

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:42.669
TCP is TCP.

00:39:42.669 --> 00:39:45.210
But I will say this is an area
where the two environments are

00:39:45.210 --> 00:39:47.220
probably the most different.

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:48.199
And I don't even--

00:39:48.199 --> 00:39:48.990
well, I don't know.

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:50.860
My team will pick one
that's better or worse.

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:52.920
I don't even that it's better or
worse, they're just different.

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:55.167
And it's helpful to know
what some of those are.

00:39:55.167 --> 00:39:57.000
Probably the biggest
thing for us-- and this

00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:57.749
is getting better.

00:39:57.749 --> 00:40:00.600
I mean, I talked a little bit
about the need for the VPNs,

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:01.890
just how to set those up.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:05.455
Like, VPNs are set up different
in AWS than they are in GCP.

00:40:05.455 --> 00:40:07.830
Once you've figured out how
to pair them and set them up,

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:09.330
they work very well.

00:40:09.330 --> 00:40:11.050
We still do a lot
of back and forth.

00:40:11.050 --> 00:40:12.633
We have a huge amount
of traffic going

00:40:12.633 --> 00:40:16.020
between the two clouds over VPN,
and that's worked well for us.

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:20.520
Google's use of SDN is
really powerful and extremely

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:23.040
performant, but causes some
things you just don't quite

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:24.810
expect sometimes.

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:26.850
For us, it had to do
with things like--

00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:29.112
first of all, the internal
load balancer at Google

00:40:29.112 --> 00:40:31.320
is actually not a load
balancer like you think of it.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:34.800
It's not like an HAProxy or some
kind of load balancer instance

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:36.225
that looks like a device.

00:40:36.225 --> 00:40:38.100
It's actually forwarding
rules in the network

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:39.690
that just moves traffic around.

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:41.850
So they call it a load
balancer, but it's really

00:40:41.850 --> 00:40:44.183
a combination of front end
and back end forwarding rules

00:40:44.183 --> 00:40:45.180
in the network.

00:40:45.180 --> 00:40:48.000
That means you can't do things
like terminate SSL at the load

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:49.344
balancer internally.

00:40:49.344 --> 00:40:51.510
Because it's really just
moving traffic to your back

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:52.440
end devices.

00:40:52.440 --> 00:40:54.480
All of which works fine,
it's just different.

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:57.450
And that was hard for
us to get used to.

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:01.702
Some of the internal shared--

00:41:01.702 --> 00:41:03.285
it's called shared
VPC, I keep wanting

00:41:03.285 --> 00:41:05.368
to call it XPN. [? A ?]
few Google's were probably

00:41:05.368 --> 00:41:07.230
remembered as XPN
before it became public.

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:10.710
But the shared VPC XPN stuff
was really early for us.

00:41:10.710 --> 00:41:12.720
It's still kind of early.

00:41:12.720 --> 00:41:14.850
Like, we didn't know
early on that you couldn't

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:19.260
expect to have the internal load
balancer not work across a VPN.

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:22.050
Because it's just a
device, networking device,

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:23.470
it should be available anywhere.

00:41:23.470 --> 00:41:25.200
But when you dig in, you
understand why that is.

00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:27.116
And we found ways to
sort of work around that.

00:41:27.116 --> 00:41:29.370
And that all works
very well for us.

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:31.200
I think, also, private
DNS was a thing that

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:32.970
was missing when we moved.

00:41:32.970 --> 00:41:33.935
It is being tested now.

00:41:33.935 --> 00:41:35.310
We're actively
testing and hoping

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:38.850
to move to private zone
resolution inside of Google.

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:40.952
And so all these things,
I think, work fine.

00:41:40.952 --> 00:41:41.910
They're just different.

00:41:41.910 --> 00:41:43.740
And you need to be
aware of them as you're

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:47.320
sort of thinking through how
you're going to do things.

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:49.380
So I guess in
summary, for us, look,

00:41:49.380 --> 00:41:50.947
our migration went really well.

00:41:50.947 --> 00:41:53.030
We're running a massive
amount of stuff in Google,

00:41:53.030 --> 00:41:53.738
it's going great.

00:41:53.738 --> 00:41:55.780
We're moving new
stuff over every day.

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:57.629
We're going to
maintain a two cloud--

00:41:57.629 --> 00:41:59.670
actually, I guess technically
we have some stuff.

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:04.110
And Azure will be in AWS for
our regulatory environment

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:06.890
that has to be in Gov Cloud.

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:08.745
But we're also digging in a lot.

00:42:08.745 --> 00:42:13.110
I mentioned heavy use of
Bigtable, Cloud Memory Store.

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:15.150
A lot of the new services
that are coming out,

00:42:15.150 --> 00:42:17.580
we're trying to migrate
and sort of [? embed ?] to,

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:19.320
even as we grow.

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:22.680
We are focused a lot on
Kubernetes and service mesh

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:23.740
approaches.

00:42:23.740 --> 00:42:26.160
I will say that, partly because
of this private networking

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:29.435
mismatch, partly because
of some experience

00:42:29.435 --> 00:42:30.810
we have in running
Kubernetes, we

00:42:30.810 --> 00:42:33.390
run self-hosted
Kubernetes in Google.

00:42:33.390 --> 00:42:35.430
We continue to evaluate
GK as an option.

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:37.830
But we actually have our
own Kubernetes deployment

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:39.150
that we run and manage.

00:42:39.150 --> 00:42:41.780
And that has worked well for us.

00:42:41.780 --> 00:42:45.676
And actually, we can piggyback
on a lot of GKE capabilities

00:42:45.676 --> 00:42:47.550
that have been put into
the public use for us

00:42:47.550 --> 00:42:49.800
to do things like
access underlying

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:54.300
IM roles, inner instances, and
credentialing and so forth.

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.250
A lot of work with the
move towards analytics

00:42:56.250 --> 00:42:59.730
to incorporate GPUs and TPUs
into our infrastructure,

00:42:59.730 --> 00:43:02.520
both in the Kubernetes
side and in other places.

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:05.070
And then there's just this
constant effort on our part

00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:09.349
to balance cost and speed, take
advantage of preemptible VMs,

00:43:09.349 --> 00:43:10.890
understand what the
best patterns are

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:15.390
for distributing our imagery,
and managing egress costs.

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:18.210
And so that's what keeps
us busy going forward

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:21.810
now that we've survived that
the basis of the migration.

00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:24.000
And we're really sort
of excited about that.

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:27.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

